        {
            "id": "32705783",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "20:06, 7 August 2024",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,420 bytes)",
            "change": -11,
            "summary": "(Replaced a bad reference for wikiHaus content team)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Buy an indoor TV antenna for the best results. If your goal is to get rid of your cable bill, investing in a $40-60 antenna is going to pay off big time. This is really the cleanest and safest way to get high-quality channels.<ref>https://www.consumerreports.org/antennas/is-now-the-time-to-get-a-tv-antenna/</ref> Your results will vary depending on where you live since the distance from your home to the broadcast signal will vary for each person, so look for an antenna with a {{convert|50-60|mi|km|abbr=on}} range for best results.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=z7JKPDBkz-M</ref>[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 2 Version 5.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Buy an indoor TV antenna for the best results. If your goal is to get rid of your cable bill, investing in a $40-60 antenna is going to pay off big time. This is really the cleanest and safest way to get high-quality channels.<ref>https://www.consumerreports.org/antennas/is-now-the-time-to-get-a-tv-antenna/</ref> Your results will vary depending on where you live since the distance from your home to the broadcast signal will vary for each person, so look for an antenna with a {{convert|50-60|mi|km|abbr=on}} range for best results.<ref>https://www.techradar.com/news/best-indoor-tv-antennas</ref>[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 2 Version 5.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "32686249",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "14:48, 30 July 2024",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,431 bytes)",
            "change": 39,
            "summary": "(Added expert citation.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#You could in theory, but it’s unsafe and unlikely to work. Your home wiring’s current is way too high for your TV’s coaxial port, and you may damage your TV or potentially start a fire if you connect your TV’s antenna port to an outlet.<ref>[v161396_b01]. 3 March 2021.</ref> On top of that, your home wiring is not designed to pick up broadcast frequencies, and the odds are high you wouldn’t be able to pick up channels anyway.<ref>https://blog.solidsignal.com/tutorials/can-you-use-your-homes-wiring-as-an-antenna/</ref>[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 1 Version 6.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#You could in theory, but it’s unsafe and unlikely to work. Your home wiring’s current is way too high for your TV’s coaxial port, and you may damage your TV or potentially start a fire if you connect your TV’s antenna port to an outlet. On top of that, your home wiring is not designed to pick up broadcast frequencies, and the odds are high you wouldn’t be able to pick up channels anyway.<ref>https://blog.solidsignal.com/tutorials/can-you-use-your-homes-wiring-as-an-antenna/</ref>[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 1 Version 6.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
