        {
            "id": "27686969",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "15:56, 5 February 2020",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(6,045 bytes)",
            "change": 0,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "adjustment",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Measure the distance from your TV or radio to the nearest wall outlet. Using your home wiring as an antenna requires plugging a coaxial cable into a wall outlet using an adapter. A coaxial cable transmits radio frequencies into a TV or radio antenna port. The adapter prevents an electrical signal from traveling through the cable and overloading your TV. To start, take a tape measure and see how far your TV or radio is from the nearest outlet. That way, you can buy a cable that’s long enough.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring/tips-for-coaxial-cable-wiring/</ref>[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 1 Version 5.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Measure the distance from your TV or radio to the nearest wall outlet. Using your home wiring as an antenna requires plugging a coaxial cable into a wall outlet using an adapter. A coaxial cable transmits radio frequencies into a TV or radio antenna port. The adapter prevents an electrical signal from traveling through the cable and overloading your TV. To start, take a tape measure and see how far your TV or radio is from the nearest outlet. That way, you can buy a cable that’s long enough.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring/tips-for-coaxial-cable-wiring/</ref>[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 1 Version 4.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "27641014",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "22:56, 24 January 2020",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(6,045 bytes)",
            "change": 551,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Measure the distance from your TV or radio to the nearest wall outlet. Using your home wiring as an antenna requires plugging a coaxial cable into a wall outlet using an adapter. A coaxial cable transmits radio frequencies into a TV or radio antenna port. The adapter prevents an electrical signal from traveling through the cable and overloading your TV. To start, take a tape measure and see how far your TV or radio is from the nearest outlet. That way, you can buy a cable that’s long enough.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring/tips-for-coaxial-cable-wiring/</ref>[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 1 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Buy a coaxial cable and a plug-in antenna wall adapter. Both devices have to work in tandem to pick up radio signals from your home wiring. These items are sold at electronics stores or on the internet.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 2 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Screw the coaxial cable into your TV or radio antenna port. The back of your TV or radio should have a port for the coaxial cable. This is where the cable transmits radio frequencies into. It may be labeled a few different ways, like “Antenna,” “ANT In,” or “ANT/CABLE.” Find this port and screw the coaxial cable into it by turning it clockwise.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 3 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Plug the coaxial cable into the antenna wall adapter. Wipe away any dust on the adapter unit and make sure it is completely dry before plugging in the coaxial cable. Then attach the cable to the adapter unit.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 4 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Plug the adapter into the wall outlet. This creates a connection between your home wiring and the TV or radio, and should allow you to pick up a broadcast signal. You can then start tuning your TV or radio to find stations.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set your TV to the Antenna input source. Your TV won’t pick up anything from the antenna if it’s set to Cable or another input. On your remote control, select Menu. Then scroll to the input options and select Antenna.<ref>https://youtu.be/2oU47R-RDxI?t=134</ref>[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Tune your TV or radio to pick up broadcast stations. With the adapter plugged into the wall, your home wiring should pick up TV or radio stations. Scroll through your TV or radio stations to see if you get reception.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 7.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Measure the distance from your TV or radio to the nearest wall outlet. Using your home wiring as an antenna requires plugging a coaxial cable into a wall outlet using an adapter. A coaxial cable transmits radio frequencies into a TV or radio antenna port. The adapter prevents an electrical signal from traveling through the cable and overloading your TV. To start, take a tape measure and see how far your TV or radio is from the nearest outlet. That way, you can buy a cable that’s long enough.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring/tips-for-coaxial-cable-wiring/</ref>",
                    "#Buy a coaxial cable and a plug-in antenna wall adapter. Both devices have to work in tandem to pick up radio signals from your home wiring. These items are sold at electronics stores or on the internet.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>",
                    "#Screw the coaxial cable into your TV or radio antenna port. The back of your TV or radio should have a port for the coaxial cable. This is where the cable transmits radio frequencies into. It may be labeled a few different ways, like “Antenna,” “ANT In,” or “ANT/CABLE.” Find this port and screw the coaxial cable into it by turning it clockwise.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>",
                    "#Plug the coaxial cable into the antenna wall adapter. Wipe away any dust on the adapter unit and make sure it is completely dry before plugging in the coaxial cable. Then attach the cable to the adapter unit.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>",
                    "#Plug the adapter into the wall outlet. This creates a connection between your home wiring and the TV or radio, and should allow you to pick up a broadcast signal. You can then start tuning your TV or radio to find stations.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>",
                    "#Set your TV to the Antenna input source. Your TV won’t pick up anything from the antenna if it’s set to Cable or another input. On your remote control, select Menu. Then scroll to the input options and select Antenna.<ref>https://youtu.be/2oU47R-RDxI?t=134</ref>",
                    "#Tune your TV or radio to pick up broadcast stations. With the adapter plugged into the wall, your home wiring should pick up TV or radio stations. Scroll through your TV or radio stations to see if you get reception.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "27060101",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "15:38, 10 September 2019",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(5,494 bytes)",
            "change": 227,
            "summary": "(wording improvements)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Although this is an age of digital broadcasting, you can still pick up TV and radio signals with an old-fashioned antenna. The wiring in your home can act as a giant antenna and pick up broadcast signals for your entertainment. First, you need the right equipment. By plugging a coaxial cable into a wall adapter, you can use your home wiring as an antenna. Then, tune your TV or radio to pick up broadcast stations.",
                    "#Measure the distance from your TV or radio to the nearest wall outlet. Using your home wiring as an antenna requires plugging a coaxial cable into a wall outlet using an adapter. A coaxial cable transmits radio frequencies into a TV or radio antenna port. The adapter prevents an electrical signal from traveling through the cable and overloading your TV. To start, take a tape measure and see how far your TV or radio is from the nearest outlet. That way, you can buy a cable that’s long enough.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring/tips-for-coaxial-cable-wiring/</ref>",
                    "#*The coaxial cable should plug into the antenna jack on your TV or radio, so measure from this point. It is usually on the back of the device.",
                    "#Buy a coaxial cable and a plug-in antenna wall adapter. Both devices have to work in tandem to pick up radio signals from your home wiring. These items are sold at electronics stores or on the internet.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>",
                    "#Screw the coaxial cable into your TV or radio antenna port. The back of your TV or radio should have a port for the coaxial cable. This is where the cable transmits radio frequencies into. It may be labeled a few different ways, like “Antenna,” “ANT In,” or “ANT/CABLE.” Find this port and screw the coaxial cable into it by turning it clockwise.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>",
                    "#*If your coaxial cable does not have a radio frequency (RF) connector, you’ll also need an RF adapter so the cable can connect to the antenna. See if the cable can attach to the adapter and if not, you need the RF connector. These are available at electronics stores.",
                    "#*Make sure your outlet is [[Ground-an-Outlet|properly grounded]]. You can test if your outlets are grounded by plugging in a tester unit.",
                    "*This wall adapter will not block an electrical surge from damaging your TV or radio. Unplug the adapter if there is a thunderstorm in your area."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Although we are in an age of digital broadcasting, you can still pick up TV and radio signals with an old-fashioned antenna. The wiring in your home can act as a giant antenna and pick up broadcast signals for your entertainment. First you need the right equipment. A coaxial cable plugged into a wall adapter will use your home wiring as an antenna. Then tune your TV or radio to pick up broadcast stations.",
                    "#Measure the distance from your TV or radio to the nearest wall outlet. Using your home wiring as an antenna requires plugging a coaxial cable into a wall outlet using an adapter. To start, take a tape measure and see how far your TV or radio is from the nearest outlet. That way, you can buy a cable that’s long enough.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring/tips-for-coaxial-cable-wiring/</ref>",
                    "#*The coaxial cable should plug into the antenna jack on your TV or radio, so measure from this point.",
                    "#Buy a coaxial cable and a plug-in antenna wall adapter. The coaxial cable brings an antenna signal to the TV or radio, and the wall adapter lets the cable pick up radio signals using your home wiring. These items are sold at electronics stores or on the internet.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>",
                    "#Screw the coaxial cable into your TV or radio the antenna port. The back of your TV or radio should have a port for the coaxial cable. It may be labeled a few different ways, like “Antenna,” “ANT In,” or “ANT/CABLE.” Find this port and screw the coaxial cable into it by turning it clockwise.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>",
                    "#*If your coaxial cable does not have an RF connector, you’ll also need an RF adapter so the cable can connect to the antenna. RF connectors are available at electronics stores.",
                    "#*Make sure your outlet is properly grounded. You can test if your outlets are grounded by plugging in a tester unit.<ref>https://homeguides.sfgate.com/can-tell-house-grounded-threeprong-outlet-35977.html</ref>",
                    "*This wall adapter will not be able to block an electrical surge from damaging your TV or radio. Unplug the adapter if there is a thunderstorm in your area."
                ]
            }
        },
