        {
            "id": "27055195",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "14:01, 9 September 2019",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(5,183 bytes)",
            "change": 1192,
            "summary": "(full overhaul, added steps, added methods, added sources)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Although we are in an age of digital broadcasting, you can still pick up TV and radio signals with an old-fashioned antenna. The wiring in your home can act as a giant antenna and pick up broadcast signals for your entertainment. First you need the right equipment. A coaxial cable plugged into a wall adapter will use your home wiring as an antenna. Then tune your TV or radio to pick up broadcast stations.",
                    "==Steps==",
                    "===Gathering the Necessary Equipment===",
                    "#Measure the distance from your TV or radio to the nearest wall outlet. Using your home wiring as an antenna requires plugging a coaxial cable into a wall outlet using an adapter. To start, take a tape measure and see how far your TV or radio is from the nearest outlet. That way, you can buy a cable that’s long enough.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring/tips-for-coaxial-cable-wiring/</ref>",
                    "#*The coaxial cable should plug into the antenna jack on your TV or radio, so measure from this point.",
                    "#Buy a coaxial cable and a plug-in antenna wall adapter. The coaxial cable brings an antenna signal to the TV or radio, and the wall adapter lets the cable pick up radio signals using your home wiring. These items are sold at electronics stores or on the internet.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>",
                    "#*Coaxial cables come in many sizes. Use the measurements you took to buy the correct length.",
                    "#*Plug-in wall adapters are not very popular, so you’ll probably have to order one off the internet.",
                    "#*Make sure any electrical equipment you use is UL-approved. The UL (Underwriters Laboratories) is an organization that tests electrical equipment for safety and performance. Many businesses refuse to use products that aren’t UL-approved. Look on packaging for a UL stamp of approval, or search the UL website for the product.<ref>https://marks.ul.com/about/ul-listing-and-classification-marks/</ref>",
                    "#Screw the coaxial cable into your TV or radio the antenna port. The back of your TV or radio should have a port for the coaxial cable. It may be labeled a few different ways, like “Antenna,” “ANT In,” or “ANT/CABLE.” Find this port and screw the coaxial cable into it by turning it clockwise.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>",
                    "===Finding a Broadcast Signal===",
                    "#Plug the coaxial cable into the antenna wall adapter. Wipe away any dust on the adapter unit and make sure it is completely dry before plugging in the coaxial cable. Then attach the cable to the adapter unit.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>",
                    "#*If your coaxial cable does not have an RF connector, you’ll also need an RF adapter so the cable can connect to the antenna. RF connectors are available at electronics stores.",
                    "#Plug the adapter into the wall outlet. This creates a connection between your home wiring and the TV or radio, and should allow you to pick up a broadcast signal. You can then start tuning your TV or radio to find stations.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>",
                    "#*Make sure your outlet is properly grounded. You can test if your outlets are grounded by plugging in a tester unit.<ref>https://homeguides.sfgate.com/can-tell-house-grounded-threeprong-outlet-35977.html</ref>",
                    "#*Never plug a coaxial cable into an outlet without an adapter. This will send an electrical current into your TV and could start a fire.",
                    "#Set your TV to the Antenna input source. Your TV won’t pick up anything from the antenna if it’s set to Cable or another input. On your remote control, select Menu. Then scroll to the input options and select Antenna.<ref>https://youtu.be/2oU47R-RDxI?t=134</ref>",
                    "#*Your TV might have a different process for changing the input. Consult your owner’s manual if your TV works differently.",
                    "#Tune your TV or radio to pick up broadcast stations. With the adapter plugged into the wall, your home wiring should pick up TV or radio stations. Scroll through your TV or radio stations to see if you get reception.<ref>https://itstillworks.com/12369331/how-to-use-a-satellite-dish-as-an-antenna</ref>",
                    "#*The signal will not be entirely clear because your home wiring is not optimized for broadcast reception. Still, you should get some signal.",
                    "#*Remember that an antenna will only pick up TV stations broadcast over the air. These are usually channels 2, 4, 5, 7, and 11, but may vary depending on your location.",
                    "==Warnings==",
                    "*Do not try to adjust your home’s wiring unless you are qualified to do so. Electricity is very dangerous and you can cause a fire or get electrocuted if you aren’t careful.",
                    "*Never plug a coaxial cable into a wall outlet without an adapter. This will send an electrical current into your TV and fry it. It could even start a fire.",
                    "*This wall adapter will not be able to block an electrical surge from damaging your TV or radio. Unplug the adapter if there is a thunderstorm in your area.",
                    "==References==",
                    "{{reflist}}",
                    "__PARTS__"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Despite the advance of modern digital broadcasting, there are certain instances where it could be beneficial to understand how to use your home wiring as a [[Upgrade Your Old TV|TV]] or radio antenna. For example, if you would like to receive local broadcast stations in your garage, basement, or other location without cable connectivity, utilizing your home's wiring in conjunction with a rabbit ears antenna may provide useful results. In addition, this method requires absolutely no direct connection to your home's electrical wiring, significantly reducing or even eliminating risk of electrical shock during installation or use.",
                    "== Steps ==",
                    "#Choose a location for your rabbit ears antenna that is directly above an electrical outlet in your home.[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Going in a straight line above the electrical outlet, drill two small holes in the ceiling directly above the outlet. The holes should be drilled approximately {{convert|3|to|6|in|cm|1}} from the wall on which the outlet is located, and should be approximately {{convert|8|in|cm|1}} apart.",
                    "#* Use a stud finder to locate the stud nearest to the electrical outlet. If possible, drill the ceiling holes at equal distances from the stud.",
                    "#Mount the rabbit ears antenna on the wall above the electrical outlet.[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* The rabbit ears antenna should be mounted at a height that will allow the fully extended rabbit ears to protrude several inches into the two holes which were drilled into the ceiling. Mount the antenna into the wall using a large screw, drilling directly into the stud above the electrical outlet.",
                    "#Extend the rabbit ears into the two holes in the ceiling.[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 3 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Make sure that the extended rabbit ears are sticking through the holes at least {{convert|2|to|3|in|cm|1}}, preferably even further. This will allow the rabbit ear antennas to receive the bounced signals from your home's electrical wiring.",
                    "#Turn on your antennae and TV or radio and experiment with different rabbit ear positions to optimize the signal performance.[[Image:Use Your Home Wiring as a TV or Radio Antenna Step 4 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Try various positions of the rabbit ears to find a position which provides clear broadcasting. You may want to try protruding only one rabbit ear through the ceiling holes. You may also try retracting one or both of the rabbit ears back towards the antenna.",
                    "#* The goal of trying various positions is to determine the location of the rabbit ears which will best connect to the bounced signals from your home's wiring. This may require a substantial amount of experimentation, depending on the electrical strength of the outlet you are utilizing.",
                    "== Tips ==",
                    "*If you live in a large apartment complex or multi-level building, you may be able to utilize the electrical wiring of the cable connections to increase the effectiveness of an antenna. Simply connect the antenna via a standard coaxial cable to the cable outlet on the wall. This will essentially utilize the building's cable system as a large over-the-air antenna.",
                    "*While some methods of tapping in to your home's wiring to boost your TV or radio antenna require complicated and dangerous procedures of connecting to live wires, using a simple rabbit ears antenna strategically installed near your home's wiring can provide similar and far safer results.",
                    "",
                    "== References ==",
                    "*[http://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2010/09/100915094230.htm http://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2010/09/100915094230.htm]",
                    "*[http://www.crutchfield.com/S-Zzw3CkiWGpq/learn/learningcenter/home/antenna.html http://www.crutchfield.com/S-Zzw3CkiWGpq/learn/learningcenter/home/antenna.html]"
                ]
            }
        },
